in the foothills of Albania's Alps the  remote town of Hass so many people have  migrated from Hass to the UK in recent  years there's some people like Naim feel  left behind what he calls a ghost town  he tried to reach Britain from France  himself a few years ago by hiding in a  lorry but he didn't make it  everyone I know is in the UK including  all my friends from high school there  was a time when I really wanted to go  too to see what it's like but now maybe  I'd rather just visit  it's not easy for albanians to get UK  work visas but a growing network of  traffickers have made it possible to  cross the English Channel from France  illegally by small boat it's dangerous  but young people like bugar say they  have no choice Albania is stable but  poverty and Corruption are widespread  jobs and opportunities scarce  most people have made it by boat as they  can't get a Visa as for me I don't have  a Visa so what am I supposed to do I'll  have no choice I'll have to take a boat  because there is nothing for us there's  no work people began migrating from a  house in the 1990s after the end of  Communism in Albania many ended up in  Britain creating a network of jobs and  connections that paved the way for  others going to the UK has become  something of a tradition in-house it  feels familiar in a way that other  countries don't there are reminders of  the UK everywhere in Hassan some of the  names of the cafes and restaurants all  the fact that some of the cars have  British license plates the city council  is even considering building a statue of  the queen funded by some of the money  that albanians are sending home the  statue is the mayor's idea he jokes that  Hass is almost a London suburb he says  people are grateful for the wealth  generated by migration to Britain it  fuels the local economy but the  dwindling young population is a problem  for the town I discarded the Region's  roads are so dilapidated that it's  difficult to reach many villages it's  i